          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Group Size, Terminals and CCA: How to Match a Generator Battery
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four numbers decide whether a battery will work in your generator. Find them in your generator's owner's manual, on the label of the old battery, or on the maker's parts list, and match them before you order. A battery that is the right voltage but the wrong shape or terminal layout is the most common return.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Group Size and Dimensions</h3>

          <p>
            The group size, such as 26R or U1, is a Battery Council International standard for the case's length, width and height. Your generator's battery tray, hold-down strap and cover are built around one size. For small sealed batteries with no group number, compare the length, width and height in inches with your old battery, allowing for the terminals.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Terminal Layout and Polarity</h3>

          <p>
            The R in 26R and U1R means reversed terminals: the positive post is on the opposite side from a standard 26 or U1. Generator battery cables are cut to length, so the wrong layout can leave a cable that will not reach or one stretched across the top of the battery, which is a short-circuit risk. Match the positive and negative positions to your old battery, and check the terminal type too: most small generator batteries use nut-and-bolt terminals for ring connectors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold Cranking Amps</h3>

          <p>
            Cold cranking amps, or CCA, measure how much current a battery can deliver at 0 degrees F for 30 seconds. Your manual may list a minimum; meet or beat it, especially if your outages come with winter storms. Many small sealed batteries list only amp-hours, not CCA, so for those, match the amp-hour rating and size the manual gives.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Amp-Hours and Reserve Capacity</h3>

          <p>
            Amp-hours and reserve capacity measure stored energy, not starting power. A little extra helps a battery survive the slow drain of a generator's controller or long storage, but more amp-hours never makes up for the wrong size or too few CCA. Here is how the seven batteries in this guide compare.
          </p>

