          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Best 26R Battery for a Generator: AGM or Flooded?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your home standby generator calls for a group 26R battery, the two best options here are the Mighty Max MM-G26R, a sealed AGM, and the Interstate MT-26R, a conventional flooded battery. Both meet the 26R footprint and list more than 500 cold cranking amps. The choice comes down to chemistry, warranty and where you buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why AGM Suits Most Standby Generators</h3>

          <p>
            An absorbed glass mat battery is sealed, does not need water and is far less likely to leak acid onto the generator's controller or enclosure. AGM batteries generally resist vibration and self-discharge better than flooded ones, which suits a battery that is charged by a controller and asked to crank once a week. Some generator makers specify a particular type, so follow your manual.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Flooded 26R Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            A flooded battery is the traditional design. It usually costs less and is widely sold, and a store-backed brand makes warranty swaps and core returns simple. The trade-off is that it vents hydrogen gas while charging, must stay upright and may need its electrolyte checked if it has removable caps. Inside a ventilated standby enclosure that is fine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Manual's Minimum CCA</h3>

          <p>
            Standby generator manuals typically list the group size and a minimum CCA rating. Cold weather is when a standby generator is most needed and when a battery has the least cranking power, so do not buy a 26R with a lower CCA than your manual asks for. If you live somewhere with harsh winters, choose the higher of the options that fit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. AGM vs Flooded vs Gel vs Lithium for Generators
          </h2>

          <p>
            Generator batteries come in four chemistries. The right one is usually the one your manual names, but it helps to know why.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">AGM</h3>

          <p>
            Sealed AGM batteries are the default for portable generators and a popular choice for standby units. They are spill-proof, mount in most positions and vent very little gas in normal use, though they still have safety valves. Six of the seven batteries in this guide are sealed designs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flooded</h3>

          <p>
            Flooded lead-acid batteries have liquid electrolyte. They are cheaper and proven but must be mounted upright and kept in a ventilated space, because charging releases hydrogen, which is explosive in a confined area. Never charge one in a closed room or next to a spark or flame.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gel</h3>

          <p>
            Gel batteries suspend the electrolyte in a gel. Makers claim strong vibration resistance and long standby life. They can be more sensitive to high charging voltages, so use a charger with a gel or AGM setting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lithium</h3>

          <p>
            Lithium iron phosphate starter batteries are lighter and hold their charge well, but some generator charging circuits are not designed for them and some will not charge them in freezing weather. Unless your generator maker approves lithium, stay with lead-acid.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Replace a Generator Battery Safely
          </h2>

          <p>
            Replacing a generator battery takes about fifteen minutes with a wrench or socket set. Follow your generator maker's instructions, which take priority over these general steps, and wear safety glasses throughout: lead-acid batteries contain sulfuric acid and can release explosive gas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Shut Down and Isolate the Generator</h3>

          <p>
            Turn the generator off and let it cool. On a standby generator, switch the controller to off or manual and remove its fuse or open its breaker as the manual directs, so the unit cannot try to start while you work. Disconnect any battery charger.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Disconnect the Negative Cable First</h3>

          <p>
            Remove the negative (black) cable first, then the positive (red). Taking the negative off first means a wrench that touches the frame while you loosen the positive will not cause a spark. Keep metal tools and jewelry away from both posts at once.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Swap the Battery and Clean the Terminals</h3>

          <p>
            Lift out the old battery, keeping it upright, and check the cable ends for white or green corrosion. Clean them with a wire brush and a baking soda solution if needed. Set the new battery in the same orientation and fit the hold-down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Connect Positive First, Negative Last</h3>

          <p>
            Reconnect the positive cable first and the negative cable last, and tighten both firmly without over-torquing small terminals. Put the terminal covers back on. Then restore the controller fuse or breaker, return a standby unit to auto, and run a test start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="charging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Do Generator Batteries Need a Trickle Charger?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A charged battery is the difference between a key start and a pull cord in the dark. Whether you need a separate charger depends on the generator.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Portable Generators</h3>

          <p>
            Most electric-start portables recharge their battery only while running, and a battery left for months loses charge on its own. A battery maintainer, sometimes called a smart trickle charger, is the best way to keep it ready. Some generators have a charging port for exactly this. Our guide to the <a href="/best-battery-maintainer/">best battery maintainers</a> covers the chargers, including models with AGM and lithium modes and small-battery settings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Home Standby Generators</h3>

          <p>
            Standby generators have a built-in battery charger in the controller, so they do not need an external trickle charger. If the battery keeps going flat, the problem is usually an old battery or a charger fault, which a dealer should check.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choosing a Maintainer for a Generator Battery</h3>

          <p>
            Pick a smart maintainer that matches your battery's chemistry and stops at full charge, not an old-style charger that keeps pushing current. Its amperage matters less than its charge profile: a small sealed 9Ah battery is better served by a low-amp maintainer than a car charger.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Long Does a Generator Battery Last?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A generator battery typically lasts a few years, often three to five, but that depends far more on how it is charged and stored than on its brand. Treat the warranty length as a rough guide to the maker's confidence.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs It Is Time to Replace It</h3>

          <p>
            Watch for a slow or labored crank, a standby controller showing a low-battery or failed-start warning, a battery that will not hold charge after a full recharge, a swollen or cracked case, and heavy corrosion on the posts. A battery that is many years old and fails a load test should be replaced before the next storm season, not after it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat Shortens Life; Cold Hides the Damage</h3>

          <p>
            Heat inside a sun-baked enclosure ages a battery fastest, while cold weather reduces the cranking power it has left. That is why weak batteries so often fail on the first freezing night of an outage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Test It</h3>

          <p>
            A healthy, fully charged 12-volt lead-acid battery reads roughly 12.6 to 12.8 volts at rest on a multimeter. A reading near 12.4 volts means it needs charging, and much lower suggests a problem. A load test at an auto parts store gives a more reliable verdict on cranking health.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Storing a Generator Battery Between Outages
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most portable generator batteries die in storage, not in use. A lead-acid battery left partly discharged for months sulfates, which permanently cuts its capacity. Three habits prevent it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep It Charged or Connected</h3>

          <p>
            Either leave a smart maintainer connected through the off-season or recharge the battery every month or two. Do not store it flat. If you disconnect it, remove the negative cable first and store the battery somewhere cool and dry, away from flames and out of reach of children.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Run the Generator Regularly</h3>

          <p>
            Starting the generator monthly under a light load exercises the engine, refreshes the fuel system and puts some charge back into the battery. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-store-a-portable-generator/">how to store a portable generator</a> covers fuel, oil and battery steps for long storage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recycling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Dispose of an Old Generator Battery
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lead-acid batteries must never go in household trash. They contain lead and sulfuric acid, and they are among the most recycled products in the country.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Return It as a Core</h3>

          <p>
            Many battery sellers charge a refundable core deposit and take the old battery back; Interstate's listing includes one. Auto parts stores and battery retailers commonly accept used lead-acid batteries even without a purchase. Keep the old battery upright and carry it in a plastic tub in case it leaks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Small Sealed Batteries</h3>

          <p>
            Small sealed lead acid batteries, like the 9Ah and 18Ah sizes, are also recyclable at battery retailers, many hardware stores and household hazardous waste sites. Tape the terminals before transport so they cannot short against anything metal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by Voltage Instead of Group Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every battery here is 12 volts, but only one size fits your tray and hold-down. Match the group size or the exact dimensions in your manual.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Terminal Orientation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 26 and a 26R, or a U1 and a U1R, have their posts reversed. The wrong one leaves cables that will not reach or stretch dangerously across the battery.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking the Positive Cable Off First</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Always disconnect the negative first and reconnect it last, so a wrench touching the frame cannot cause a spark at the battery.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing the Battery Flat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A discharged lead-acid battery sulfates in storage. Keep it on a smart maintainer or recharge it every month or two.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging a Flooded Battery Indoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flooded batteries vent hydrogen while charging. Charge them in a ventilated area away from sparks and flames, and wear eye protection.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

