        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best battery for a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best battery for a generator is the size your manual specifies. For home standby generators that take a group 26R, the Mighty Max MM-G26R is our pick: a sealed AGM listed at 540 CCA with a 3-year warranty. For electric-start portables, match the size: the Mighty Max ML18-12, UPLUS U1 or a YTX14-BS battery like the Chrome S Pro iGel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best 26R battery for a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Mighty Max MM-G26R is the best 26R battery for most standby generators: sealed AGM, 540 CCA, 45Ah and an 80-minute reserve, per its listing, with a 3-year warranty. If you prefer a flooded battery from a brand with stores and core returns, the Interstate MT-26R lists 550 CCA and 90 minutes of reserve. Check your manual's minimum CCA first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size battery does my generator take?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Your generator's owner's manual lists the battery group size or dimensions, voltage, terminal layout and sometimes a minimum CCA. The label on the old battery shows the same details. Home standby generators commonly use a group 26R, while portables use anything from a 12V 9Ah sealed battery to a group U1. Match the size and terminal orientation exactly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a trickle charger for my generator battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a portable generator, yes, unless you run it regularly. Its battery only charges while the engine runs and drains in storage, so a smart battery maintainer keeps it ready; our best battery maintainer guide covers good ones. Home standby generators have a built-in charger in the controller and do not need a separate trickle charger.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a car battery in a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it is the group size your generator specifies. Home standby generators are designed for car-style batteries such as a group 26R, so a matching car battery works. Portable generators usually use small sealed batteries, and a car battery will not fit their tray or mounts. Never connect an oversized battery with jumper leads or improvised cables.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an AGM or flooded battery better for a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most generators, AGM is the better choice. It is sealed, spill-proof, needs no water and resists vibration and self-discharge. A flooded battery costs less and works well in a ventilated standby enclosure, but it vents hydrogen while charging and must stay upright. Follow your generator manual if it specifies a type.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a generator battery last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generator batteries typically last about three to five years, depending on heat, how well they are kept charged and how long they sit discharged. Replace a battery that cranks slowly, will not hold a charge, fails a load test or shows a swollen case before storm season, rather than waiting for it to fail during an outage.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
